$e^{3 \pi i /4} = (\cos(3 \pi /4) + i\sin(3 \pi /4)) = (-1/\sqrt{2} +(1/\sqrt{2})i)$. So then we have
\begin{align*}
\sqrt{2} (-1/\sqrt{2} +(1/\sqrt{2})i ) & & \text{since we have out whole multiplication again.} \\
& = -1 + i
\end{align*}
